  "worked_solution": "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n2(5)^{x}-50=0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by adding $50$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(5)^{x}-50 & =0 \\\\ 2(5)^{x}-\\cancel{50}+\\cancel{50} & =0+50 \\\\ 2(5)^{x} & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(5)^{x} & =50 \\\\ {5}^{x} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite $25$ as ${5}^{2}$ to get\n\n$$\n{5}^{x}={5}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(5)$, we equate the exponents. This gives\n\n$$\nx=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=2.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
